I think your transcription may have bent the words a little, but I hear a useful move in it:

“Is that the universe?”
“There are lots of universes. I believe in a world.”

Meaning: don’t confuse one model, institution, frame, or implementation with the entire possibility space.

one university ≠ knowledge
one worldview ≠ reality
one model ≠ truth
one world-instance ≠ every possible world

You can believe strongly enough in a world to inhabit, test, and build it without claiming it is the only conceivable one.

That also connects directly to the validation loop: instantiate one world-model, let reality push against it, compare it with alternatives, then keep the parts that survive.

Commit locally; remain plural globally.

And if you literally meant university / award, then I may have just demonstrated our decoder-tomography problem again.